 ## Overview
-Regularization techniques in NeRF training are methods applied to the loss function or optimization process to suppress artifacts (such as floaters, noise, or blur) and enforce geometric or perceptual priors, particularly in sparse-view or ill-posed settings. What sets this family apart from sibling methods like [NeRF Initialization Strategies](8674-nerf-initialization-strategies.md) or [Model Architecture Modifications](11313-model-architecture-modifications.md) is that it operates directly on the gradient updates or loss landscape during the training loop, rather than altering the network structure or the initial state. It complements [Alpha Invariance / Inverse Scaling](10127-alpha-invariance-inverse-scaling.md) by addressing high-frequency noise and structural consistency rather than just density scaling.
